    {
      "id": "ledger-notes",
      "kind": {
        "text": "Genuinely open mathematics, so the empty evidence array is the honest record. Worth being precise about what a solver can and cannot contribute: each bounded instance `n = 2k` is a finite check the arithmetic route decides, but no finite family of decided instances is evidence for the quantified statement, and the schema offers no way to record `verified for all n below 4e18` other than as a separate, weaker fact. formal.statement is written in the SAME core rendering the kernel emits for its own theorems, and uses ONLY symbols `build_nat_prelude` actually declares (checked against the 119-theorem inventory dumped on 2026-08-14), so it can be dispatched without inventing vocabulary. Primality is spelled out inline because the prelude has no `Prime` predicate. Binders carry readable names rather than the kernel's `x0, x1, ...`; that is a pretty-printing difference only, and any admitted proof would render with positional names.",
        "type": "prose"
      },
      "provenance": {
        "command": "python3 render/producers-py/facts_to_docir.py",
